{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"mcporter call exa.web_search_exa query='site:forum.intervals.icu October 2026 Garmin heart rate workout range zone target export start end' numResults=8\"","aggregated_output":"Title: Suunto guides: heart-rate step targets show no target and no gauge on the watch, pace targets do\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/suunto-guides-heart-rate-step-targets-show-no-target-and-no-gauge-on-the-watch-pace-targets-do/131169\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n## What happens\n\nOn a workout synced to a Suunto 9 Peak, a step whose target is pace shows the step\nname, `Avg`, `Target`, the value and the gauge. A step whose target is heart rate\nshows the step name and the ordinary running fields only – no target, no range, no\ngauge, nothing that says what to hold.\n\nI could not tell from the watch whether this was my syntax, so I built one workout with\neleven steps, one target form each, and walked through all of them:\n\n| step | written as | on the watch |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 zone | `90s Z3 HR` | no target, no gauge |\n| 2 pctmax one | `90s 85% HR` | no target, no gauge |\n| 3 pctmax rng | `90s 82-87% HR` | no target, no gauge |\n| 4 thr one | `90s 90% LTHR` | no target, no gauge |\n| 5 thr rng | `90s 88-94% LTHR` | no target, no gauge |\n| 6 zone span | `90s Z2-Z3 HR` | no target, no gauge |\n| 7 zone p | `90s Z2 Pace` | Avg + Target + gauge |\n| 8 pct p one | `90s 40% Pace` | Avg + Target + gauge |\n| 9 pct p rng | `90s 60-70% Pace` | Avg + Target + gauge |\n| 10 abs p | `90s 7:00 pace` | Avg + Target + gauge |\n| 11 dist | `400mtr Z2 Pace` | Avg + Target + gauge |\n\nAll six heart-rate forms behave identically. All five pace forms work.\n\n## Why I think the data is not the problem\n\nIntervals resolves every one of those HR steps to absolute bpm – they are shown that way\nin the workout view, and they are in the workout export\n(`/api/v1/athlete/{id}/events/{id}/download.json`) as a `_hr` object:\n\n```auto\n1 zone \"_hr\": {\"start\": 153.0, \"end\": 163.0}\n2 pctmax one \"_hr\": {\"value\": 159.0, \"start\": 156.0, \"end\": 162.0}\n3 pctmax rng \"_hr\": {\"value\": 158.5, \"start\": 154.0, \"end\": 163.0}\n4 thr one \"_hr\": {\"value\": 154.0, \"start\": 151.0, \"end\": 157.0}\n5 thr rng \"_hr\": {\"value\": 156.0, \"start\": 151.0, \"end\": 161.0}\n6 zone span \"_hr\": {\"start\": 146.0, \"end\": 163.0}\n\n```\n\nAnd the SuuntoPlus Guide format has a field for exactly this shape. From Suunto’s own\nGuide Cloud API document, the sample guide’s warm-up step:\n\n```json\n\"fields\": [\n  { \"type\": \"targetHeartRate\", \"value\": 111, \"min\": 104, \"max\": 148 },\n  { \"type\": \"heartRate\" },\n  ...\n]\n\n```\n\n`targetHeartRate` takes absolute bpm as a value with optional min/max, and Suunto’s own\nexample uses it on a heart-rate step.\n\n## And the watch can display it\n\nA structured workout built in the Suunto app on the same watch, with heart-rate bands\n(warm-up 130-140, interval 150-160, recovery 100-110), shows the target and the gauge\nnormally during the heart-rate steps. So the watch and its target display are not the\nlimitation.\n\n...\n\nThat was it !!!\n\nthank you! I built a workout with ONLY heart-rate steps and all three show\nthe target and the dial, perfectly.\n\n...\n\nYou could try to set it in Intervals as ‘Auto’.\nI don’t know if a mix will then work in Suunto, but there was recently some work done to get this supported on Garmin.\n\nIt’s strange that we don’t see those selectors on your screenshot. If you select HR, the Intervals graph should show the workout coloured in HR zones. If Pace is selected, it would be in Pace zone colors. Auto will show it depending on your priority settings.\n\n---\n\nTitle: HR target workouts broken on Edge 840 firmware ≥30.18 — interpreted as %HRmax instead of bpm - Bug Reports - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/hr-target-workouts-broken-on-edge-840-firmware-30-18-interpreted-as-hrmax-instead-of-bpm/130090\nPublished: 2026-05-19T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n## Summary\n\nAfter installing Edge 840 firmware 30.18 (changelog entry: “Aligned presentation of time in zones for power and heart rate with Garmin Connect”),\nstructured workouts pushed via the intervals.icu API with HR-bpm targets are misinterpreted by the device.\nThe Edge now displays the workout’s lower HR bound as `bpm_value × HRmax / 100`.\nFor an athlete with HR max 199 and a target of 122 bpm, the Edge alarms `\"HR below minimum 242 bpm\"` (= 122 % × 199 ≈ 242.8) continuously throughout the ride.\n\nThe same workout structure worked correctly on the Edge before the firmware update.\n\n## Reproduction\n\nPush a Z2 workout with HR-bpm target via `/events/bulk`:\n\n```bash\ncurl -X POST 'https://intervals.icu/api/v1/athlete/0/events/bulk?upsert=true&upsertOnUid=false&updatePlanApplied=false' \\\n  -u \"API_KEY:$KEY\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" -d '[{\n    \"external_id\": \"hr-bug-repro\",\n    \"category\": \"WORKOUT\",\n    \"type\": \"Ride\",\n    \"start_date_local\": \"2026-05-25T09:00:00\",\n    \"name\": \"HR bug repro\",\n    \"moving_time\": 3600,\n    \"description\": \"- 60m 122-147bpm Z2 endurance\",\n    \"workout_doc\": {\n      \"target\": \"HR\",\n      \"steps\": [{\n        \"intensity\": \"active\",\n        \"duration\": 3600,\n        \"hr\": {\"start\": 122, \"end\": 147, \"units\": \"bpm\"},\n        \"text\": \"Z2 endurance\"\n      }]\n    }\n  }]'\n\n```\n\nLoad the synced workout on an Edge 840 running firmware ≥30.18 and start the activity. The device alarms `\"HR below minimum 242 bpm\"` (where 242 = `122 × HRmax / 100`).\n\n## Investigation findings\n\nWhile debugging this on my own account, I observed that all public export endpoints strip HR-bpm targets entirely:\n\n| Export | HR-bpm result | Power-%FTP result (control) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `/events/{id}/download.zwo` | `` — no target attrs | `` |\n| `/events/{id}/download.mrc` | Empty `[COURSE DATA]` | Populated with target % values |\n| `/events/{id}/download.fit` | 195 bytes, no target zone | 204 bytes, with power target |\n\nThe `hr_zone` variant (`hr: {units: \"hr_zone\", value: 2}`) also strips on export — even though intervals.icu maintains a distinct HR-zone internal model (`zoneTimes` returns Z1–Z5 instead of the power-zone Z1–Z7 when `target: HR`).\n\nAdditionally, intervals.icu’s own Zwift importer drops HR targets : uploading a `.zwo` containing `` via `/folders/{id}/import-workout` results in a stored `workout_doc` where the step is replaced by `power: {units: \"%ftp\", value: 0}` — the HR target metadata is discarded entirely.\n\nThese observations suggest the `workout_doc` HR-target representation either has no Garmin-format translation in your codebase, or the internal Garmin Connect sync API path emits HR values without an explicit absolute-bpm unit marker — relying on a pre-30.18 lenient Edge default that no longer exists.\n\n## Suspected cause\n\nintervals.icu’s Garmin Connect sync emits HR target values without explicitly tagging them as absolute bpm. Pre-30.18, the Edge defaulted to “treat as bpm.” Post-30.18, the Edge follows Garmin Connect’s documented convention and defaults to “treat as %HRmax.” The numeric value passes through unchanged, but its meaning flips.\n\n## Workaround (for affected users)\n\nSwitch HR-target workouts to power-target with `{units: \"%ftp\"}` ranges — these survive both the export pipeline and the Garmin Connect sync correctly. For HR-ceiling enforcement on the Edge, use the device-level HR alert (Activity Profile → Alerts → Heart Rate → set ceiling), which works regardless of workout target.\n\n...\n\nThis is no valid intervals syntax. If you use the API the description should contain valid syntax, this is what will be parsed.\n\nAn example of correct syntax would be:\n\n```auto\n\"description\": \"- 122-147bpm 60m Z2 HR\",\n\n```\n\nBut intervals is not supporting absolute values. The bpm range is considered as text.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Garmin planned workout sync — multi-target steps & zone-only targets - Feature Requests - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/garmin-planned-workout-sync-multi-target-steps-zone-only-targets/130551\nPublished: 2026-06-29T17:42:31.000Z\nAuthor: Il_Jae_Lee\nHighlights:\nTwo related enhancements to how planned workouts sync to Garmin that would make structured sessions translate more faithfully on the watch:\n\n1. Multi-target steps (e.g. HR and Power)\n\nCurrently a workout step syncs with a single target type. It would be great to attach more than one target to the same step — for example a Power primary target with a secondary HR cap/ceiling, or a Power target plus an HR guardrail. This is especially useful for easy/long runs where I want to govern by HR but keep a power ceiling, and for quality work where I want power as the lead with HR as a sanity check. Garmin’s own workout format supports a target plus a secondary/“do not exceed” constraint, so this would mostly be a matter of passing the second target through on sync.\n\n2. Zone-only targets instead of explicit ranges\n\nWhen a step is built against a zone (e.g. “Z2”), it would be ideal to sync it to Garmin as that zone reference rather than expanding it to a fixed min/max range. Two benefits: (a) the watch displays the zone the athlete actually trains by, and (b) if zone boundaries change (FTP/LTHR update), the workout stays correct without rebuilding it. Right now a hardcoded range can drift out of sync with current zones.\n\nBoth would meaningfully improve fidelity between the Intervals.icu calendar and what shows up on the Garmin head unit. Thanks for the great work.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Custom Heart Rate range planned workout - Training - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/custom-heart-rate-range-planned-workout/72460\nPublished: 2024-07-23T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nYou need to specify the HR range in % of threshold HR. This is so workouts are portable between athletes. So if threshold HR is 170 bpm then use 130/170 = 76%. So “- 10m 74-76% HR”.\n\n...\n\nAlso possible with % of maxHR (which could give a slightly wider range of bpm).\n\n...\n\nThank you, I thought about that, just wondering whether absolute values were an option.\nThe question came because in Friel´s book “Fast after 50” there is a heart rate based workout that uses this formula:\nInterval Limits = LTHR - 30 +/- 2\n\nI may also create the workout in Garmin Connect that alows to enter BPM targets.\n\n...\n\nTry not get fixated on an abolute value, as your physiology doesn’t work on on/off switches. It can change day to day, depending on your sleep, hydration, temprature (cooling) and more. Having said that, for the aerobic threshold test/workout that he refers to, limit +/-2bpm, you can use %maxHR, like follows:\n\n-30m 70-71% HR\n\nThis will be 2-3 bpm, maybe 2-4 if the maxHR is low.\nI’m using example values only.\n\nGo ride, and see how you do.\n\nIf testing, it must be the same route, each time, so you have a comparison to the benchmark.\nBut note that power and RPE will fluctuate irrespective of how you feel, due to the wind, elevation, temperature, hydration, sleep all affecting HR.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Import workouts from fit file does not work with absolute HR or pace\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/import-workouts-from-fit-file-does-not-work-with-absolute-hr-or-pace/92909\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nAs per title. Workouts for garmin can include absolute values both for pace and heart rate similar to absolute values for Watts in cycling activities. These are currently read in as %LTHR and % Pace respectively. The data range for the Heart Rates is wrong as , leading to for example 124-140% LTHR when the boundaries for that are actually 124-140 BPM. Similarly the import of absolute pace seems to convert to % Pace, which looks more reasonable, but when actually synched to the calendar and used for training the device (Fenix 7) does not show any boundaries, only reports the correct step duration. Uploading the .fit files directly to the watch works without problems with the expected behaviour.\n\n...\n\nIntervals.icu will convert absolute BPM values into %LTHR and speed (pace) into % of threshold pace. This conversion is done using the settings for the selected athlete. If you send me (david@intervals.icu) some examples of things not working I will have a look.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Garmin Workout Steps & Notes from Intervals.icu\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/garmin-workout-steps-notes-from-intervals-icu/121993\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n> ENHANCER\n> \n> You are a workout generator that outputs correctly formatted Intervals.icu’s “Structured Text Workout Format.”.\n> \n> ⚙ Format rules:\n> \n> 1. Section headers like Warmup/Main Set/Cooldown on a separate line.\n> 2. Steps begin with `- `, followed by:\n> • Duration (e.g., `5m`, `30s`, `1m30`) OR Distance (e.g., `1km`, `0.200km`, `200 meters`).\n> • Target intensity - for swimming use Zone Pace, for cycling use Zone Power, for running use Zone HR :\n> – For cycling: `%` of FTP (e.g., `85%`, `95-105%`).\n> – For running: `%` of pace threshold (e.g., `95%`, `90-100%`).\n> – Zones: e.g., `Z2`, `Z3 HR`, or `Z2 Pace`, or `Z2 Power`\n> \n> - Zone Range: e.g., `Z1-Z2`, `Z4-Z3 HR`, or `Z1-Z2 Pace`\n> • Optional cadence (e.g., `90rpm`, `50-100rpm`).\n> • Optional ramp (e.g., `ramp 60-80%`).\n> • Optional Intesity (e.g., `intensity=active`, `intensity=interval`, `intensity=rest`, `intensity=recovery`,`intensity=warmup`, `intensity=cooldown`).\n> • Optional Swiming Drill Type (e.g., `kick`, `pull`, `drill`.\n> \n> 1. Repeats : use `Nx` on its own line, preceding the block.\n> 2. Comments/prompts free text at the begining as short summary of training and a the end of line\n> 3. Leave blank lines between sections and repeats.\n> 4. You cannot used mix modes (e.g., Power/Pace/HR).\n> 5. Write output in codeblock\n> \n> 💡 Example (cycling):\n> \n> # GOAL: 70.3 Race Simulation. Building fatigue resistance.\n> \n> # FUELING: You will burn ~2100 kJ. Eat ~60-80g Carbs/hour (Total ~180g+).\n> \n> # SPINE: Stand up for 30s every 15 mins to reset L5/S1 pressure.\n> \n> Warmup\n> \n> - 19m 70% FTP 90rpm intensity=warmup (Aerobic Activation: Deep breathing. Open the lungs.)\n> \n> Tempo Block 1 (The Engine Builder)\n> \n> - 39m 85% FTP 85-95rpm intensity=interval (Target: 204W. Smooth circles. Stand at min 15 & 30.)\n> \n> Recovery & Reset\n> \n> - 8m 40% FTP 85rpm intensity=recovery (Drink bottle 1. McKenzie Extension stretch on bike.)\n> \n> Tempo Block 2 (Short Sustained)\n> \n> - 8m 85% FTP 85-95rpm intensity=interval (Target: 204W. Lock in the rhythm.)\n> \n> Quick Reset\n> \n> - 4m 40% FTP 85rpm intensity=recovery (Shake out shoulders.)\n> \n> Tempo Block 3 (Mid-Ride Fatigue)\n> \n> - 26m 85% FTP 85-95rpm intensity=interval (Target: 204W. Focus on pulling up to engage hamstrings.)\n> \n> Major Recovery Bridge\n> \n> - 16m 40% FTP 85rpm intensity=recovery (Eat Solid Food here. Mental reset.)\n> - 8m ramp 60-75% FTP 90rpm intensity=interval (Ramp back up. Pre-fatigue for the final block.)\n> \n> The Race Simulation (The Killer Block)\n> \n> - 48m 85% FTP 85-95rpm intensity=interval (Target: 204W. This simulates km 60-90 of the bike leg. It will hurt. Hold form.)\n> \n> Cooldown & Flush\n> \n> - 15m 40% FTP 85rpm intensity=cooldown (Spin very easy. No torque.)\n> - 10m 70% FTP 90rpm intensity=interval (Final aerobic check. Keep HR stable.)\n> \n> Example (Running)\n> Run: Mixed Threshold & Speed (HR Based)\n> WU: 10m Aerobic + Drills (Press Lap)\n> MS1: 3x 8m @ Threshold HR\n> MS2: 4x 1m Accels (Ramp HR)\n> CD: 10m Easy\n> Total Duration: ~60 min\n> \n> Warmup\n> \n> - 10m Z1-Z2 HR intensity=warmup (Easy jog, build to aerobic baseline)\n> - press lap Z1 HR intensity=warmup (Stop for Dynamic Drills. Press Lap when ready to start.)\n> \n> Main Set 1 3x\n> \n> - 8m Z4 HR intensity=interval (Threshold: Hard but sustainable. Keep HR steady.)\n> - 2m Z1 HR intensity=recovery (Jog recovery, keep moving)\n> \n> Main Set 2 4x\n> \n> - 1m ramp Z4-Z5 HR intensity=interval (Accelerations: Push HR up to max)\n> - 1m Z1 HR intensity=recovery (Walk or very slow jog)\n> \n> Cooldown\n> \n> - 10m Z1 HR intensity=cooldown (Flush out legs)\n> \n> Example (Swimming)\n> Swim: 3400 Meters\n> WU: 600m (Swim + Kick)\n> Drills: 200m High Elbow\n> Pull: 400m Strength (Paddles + Buoy)\n> MS: 1600m Endurance\n> Tech: 400m Mixed Drills\n> CD: 200m Easy\n> \n> Warm Up\n> \n> - 0.4km Z1 Pace (Rozplavání: Dlouhý záběr, klidná voda)\n> - 0.2km Z1 Pace Kick (Nohy s deskou: Soustřeď se na neprolamování kolen)\n> \n> Drills 4x\n> \n> - 0.05km Z1 Pace Drill (Cvičení dle výběru: Důraz na vysoký loket - High Elbow)\n> - 15s intensity=rest\n> \n> Pull Strength 2x\n> \n> - 0.2km Z2 Pace Pull (Packy + Piškot: Síla paží, udržuj boky vysoko)\n> - 30s intensity=rest\n> \n> Main Set 4x\n> \n> - 0.4km Z2 Pace\n> - 45s intensity=rest (Pauza: Vydýchej, srovnej tep)\n> \n> Technique 8x\n> \n> - 0.05km Z1 Pace Drill (1. 6/1/6, 2. Catch-up, 3. Finger drag, 4. Perfect Swim - opakovat 2x)\n> - 15s intensity=rest\n> \n> Cool Down\n> \n> - 0.2km Z1 Pace (Vyplavání: Totální uvolnění)\n\n...\n\nYou can sync your intervals.icu calendar directly to Garmin, that way you don’t need to export the files and import them on Garmin. Works flawlessly, and the workouts just sync up instantly.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Power and HR targets for Garmin workouts\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/power-and-hr-targets-for-garmin-workouts/2926\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nYou can now specify power and HR targets for workout steps (default is lap power and lap HR):\n\nThis sorts out the issue with HR steps not working on Garmin watches (well at least some of them). You need to choose “Instant HR” (hr=1s in the text):\n\n- warmup 30s 50% LTHR hr=1s\n- Wibble 20m 78% LTHR hr=1s\n- cooldown 30s 40% LTHR hr=1s\n\n...\n\nUnfortunately the target in the Garmin API is just “PACE”. There aren’t other options like there are for power.\n\n...\n\nI ... ” ... , ... power range ... , ... configure ... push ... to\n\n...\n\n```auto\nWarmup\n- Course facile 12m 70-80% FTP\n\nActivation 2x\n- Activation 1m 97-103% FTP\n- Récup 2m 70-80% FTP\n\nTransition\n- Transition 2m 70-80% FTP\n\nMain Set 5x\n- 15s 115-130% FTP\n- Descente 15s Press Lap 65-80% FTP\n- 20s 115-130% FTP\n- Descente 20s Press Lap 65-80% FTP\n- 30s 115-130% FTP\n- Descente 30s Press Lap 65-80% FTP\n- 20s 115-130% FTP\n- Descente 20s Press Lap 65-80% FTP\n- 15s 115-130% FTP\n- Descente 15s Press Lap 65-80% FTP\n\nCooldown\n- Retour au calme 10m 65-80% FTP\n\n```\n\n---\n\nTitle: Pace targets lost in Garmin export for API-created running workouts — steps arrive on watch as \"No Target\" (parsed correctly in workout_doc) - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/pace-targets-lost-in-garmin-export-for-api-created-running-workouts-steps-arrive-on-watch-as-no-target-parsed-correctly-in-workout-doc/130706\nPublished: 2026-07-17T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nPace targets on running workout steps are being lost somewhere in the Intervals.icu → Garmin Connect export path. We’ve verified every link we can reach, and the data is correct on the Intervals.icu side — this looks like an export issue, not a syntax issue.\n\nSetup\n\n- Workouts created via the API: `POST /api/v1/athlete/0/events/bulk?upsert=true`, `category: WORKOUT`, `type: Run`, description in the plain-text workout format, `external_id` set\n- Athlete settings: `garmin_pace_range = 2.5` (default), Garmin Connect linked with “Upload planned workouts” enabled\n- Watch: Garmin Forerunner [model 165] — happy to provide exact model/firmware\n\nDescription format used (sample):\n\n```auto\n- 2.5km 5:57-5:23/km Pace\n\n```\n\n`- 1.5km 9:06-8:14/km Pace`\n\n`- 2km 6:13-5:37/km Pace`\n\nWhat works (verified via the API):\n\nReading the event back, `workout_doc.steps` is parsed perfectly — every step carries a structured pace range:\n\n```auto\n{\"pace\":{\"start\":357,\"end\":323,\"units\":\"secs/km\"},\"distance\":2500,\"duration\":848}\n\n```\n\n21/21 steps have `pace: {start, end}` on the real workout in question.\n\nWhat fails:\n\nThe workout reaches Garmin Connect (name + description arrive; the Connect app shows the description). On the watch, the steps arrive with correct distances but every step shows “No Target” — no pace target, no alerts during the workout.\n\nWhat we’ve ruled out:\n\n- Syntax: both single-value (`5:40/km Pace`) and range (`5:45-5:35/km Pace`) formats parse into correct `workout_doc` pace objects; both arrive on the watch as “No Target”\n- Stale Garmin cache: deleting the events and re-creating them fresh (to force a full re-export rather than an update) changes nothing\n- Connect app display quirk (the `[SOLVED]` thread #87358): this is on the watch itself , in the step list and during execution, not just the phone UI\n\n...\n\nAuthor: @Razvan_Zlavog\nPost date: 17 July 2026 20:06 UTC\n\nSolved — and the cause was on our end, not intervals’. The athlete simply had no Run threshold pace set in their sport settings.\n\nWith threshold_pace unset, intervals drops the per-step pace targets from the Garmin export (it falls back to HR), so every step lands as “No Target” on the watch — even though workout_doc.steps carried correct pace ranges on the intervals side the whole time. HR-target workouts were unaffected because LTHR was set, which is why only pace workouts broke.\n\nFix: set a Run threshold pace — Settings → sport settings, or via API: PUT /api/v1/athlete/0/sport-settings/{runGroupId} with body { “threshold_pace”: \\ }. intervals then auto-populates the pace zones, and after a fresh push the pace targets appear on the watch (confirmed on a Garmin Forerunner).\n\nOne gotcha for anyone re-testing: existing events need a delete + recreate to re-trigger the Garmin export — an in-place update alone doesn’t.\n\nBig thanks to R2Tom and MedTechCD — the “Did you setup your Pace zones?” pointer in the related threads was exactly it.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"mcporter call exa.web_search_exa query='site:forum.intervals.icu \\\"October 2026\\\" \\\"HR\\\" \\\"Garmin\\\" workout export range' numResults=10\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"mcporter call exa.web_search_exa query='site:forum.intervals.icu \\\"October 2026\\\" \\\"HR\\\" \\\"Garmin\\\" workout export range' numResults=10\"","aggregated_output":"Title: Activity fields - Garmin - lacking data\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/activity-fields-garmin-lacking-data/132979\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nIt has been a couple weeks I noticed some data missing at Activity Fields. Specifically VO2 and Recovery Heart Rate extracted from Garmin.\n\nAny fix yet?\n\n...\n\n> @API access to Intervals.icu:\n> \n> Unfortunately Garmin terms and conditions have been updated and It is likely that we will have to block access to Garmin data via the API soon. We didn’t receive any announcement or notification, the new T&C’s popped up in the Garmin developer portal.\n\nFor now, Garmin filters the FIT files sent to Intervals and removes ‘Not documented’ metrics. But looks like soon we will have bigger shortcomings. @Andreas_Schnederle-W already put something awesome together as a workaround. It is really good and could just as well be a permanent solution, all-though not as an official part of Intervals.\n\n...\n\n> @Garmin-intervals-bridge – your complete Garmin FIT files and wellness data in Intervals.icu, with or without the official Garmin integration:\n> \n> Update 9 Oct: Garmin has changed its partner terms and David expects Intervals will have to block Garmin-sourced data in its API ([API thread](API access to Intervals.icu - #752 by david)). Files you upload yourself are most likely not affected (“likely ok” – [David](API access to Intervals.icu - #759 by david)). The bridge’s upload mode does exactly that, verified live today: official import off, every new activity’s original, unfiltered FIT uploaded as your own file, Intervals computes Stamina…\n\n...\n\nThanks for the kind words – and agreed, that’s exactly how it should be. Intervals is an official Garmin partner, so it can’t offer anything that works around Garmin’s rules without putting at risk the integration everyone relies on. Something like the bridge has to come from outside: a private, independent open-source project that each of us runs for our own data, with our own Garmin login – nothing Intervals ships, hosts or endorses. And at least as long as Garmin doesn’t come down hard on tools like this, that’s a perfectly good place for it to live 😉\n\n...\n\nIs this why my custom fields are missing on the last three workouts?\nThe last one with complete data was Oct 8.\n\n...\n\nAuthor: @Andreas_Schnederle-W\nPost date: 14 October 2026 08:36 UTC\n\n@Samantha_Lane yes, almost certainly. The copy Garmin sends to Intervals no longer carries the fields Garmin doesn’t document (VO2max, Recovery HR, Stamina, Training Effect …), so the custom fields built on them stay empty – nothing you did.\n\nSince you already have the bridge: it puts them back from the original file for every new activity once it’s allowed to write (`--apply`, or “write to Intervals” in setup). For the ones already missing, run this once:\n`garmin-intervals-bridge backfill --scope activities --from 2026-10-08`\nIt shows what it would write. If that looks right, run the same with `--apply` at the end. For a single activity, the web page’s Actions → One activity again does the same. And the Firefox “foreign form” problem is fixed in 0.11.1, out now.\n\nAs for the wording – I’d go with “disappointing”. Intervals has to stay on good terms with Garmin for the official integration we all rely on, so let’s keep the forum friendly. 😉\n\n---\n\nTitle: Garmin-intervals-bridge – your complete Garmin FIT files and wellness data in Intervals.icu, with or without the official Garmin integration (now also with Garmin’s Personal API)\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/garmin-intervals-bridge-your-complete-garmin-fit-files-and-wellness-data-in-intervals-icu-with-or-without-the-official-garmin-integration-now-also-with-garmin-s-personal-api/132863\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nUpdate 9 Oct: Garmin has changed its partner terms and David expects Intervals will have to block Garmin-sourced data in its API ([API thread](API access to Intervals.icu - #752 by david)). Files you upload yourself are most likely not affected (“likely ok” – [David](API access to Intervals.icu - #759 by david)). The bridge’s upload mode does exactly that, verified live today: official import off, every new activity’s original, unfiltered FIT uploaded as your own file, Intervals computes Stamina, Recovery Time, VO₂max, Performance Condition, Training Effect, Sweat Loss and your other custom items from the complete file itself. Nothing flagged as edited, and apps you authorize keep seeing your activities. Docs: README → “Upload mode”.\n\n...\n\nTL;DR – Since late September Garmin filters its own metrics out of the FIT files it sends to Intervals (Stamina, Recovery Time, VO₂max, Performance Condition, Training Effect, Sweat Loss …). This open-source tool fetches the original file from Garmin Connect and adds the missing data to the activity the official sync already created – no duplicates, nothing deleted, training load untouched. It also syncs the daily wellness values the official sync doesn’t deliver (night SpO₂, respiration, sleeping HR, Body Battery, HRV details, sleep stages, stress, readiness, nutrition, total daily burn, scores, race predictions, fitness age …), can backfill the past, and brings 19 ready-made charts in the chart library (search “Garmin Bridge”). Linux, container, or Windows with one command. MIT, self-hosted, your data stays with you.\n\n...\n\n- The official Garmin → Intervals sync stays switched on. The bridge polls Intervals once a minute (one tiny request), and when a new Garmin activity appears it downloads the original FIT through Garmin Connect (byte-identical to the browser export – verified), compares it with the file Intervals received, and writes only what is missing via `PUT /activity/{id}` and `PUT /activity/{id}/streams`.\n- It fills your custom fields and streams: it reads your own custom item definitions (`fit_session_field`, `fit_record_field`, the `m.f_138` stream scripts) and takes the values from the original. Nothing Garmin-specific is hard-coded, so the public community fields (Recovery Time, Stamina, VO2MaxGarmin, Sweatloss …) just work. A stream is only written after the alignment has been proven on a stream both sides already have (heart rate).\n\n- Wellness: 22 Garmin daily endpoints, mapped into Intervals’ native fields where they exist (spO2, respiration, sleeping HR, weight, body fat, kcal consumed, carbs/protein/fat …) and into `Garmin…` custom fields for the rest. Values already present are never overwritten; locked days are skipped; today’s running totals wait until the day is over.\n- Backfill: `backfill --scope wellness --from 2025-01-01 --apply` walks the past at a polite pace (on my account night SpO₂ had silently stopped arriving in July 2025 – it’s all back now). Activities from the past the same way – and in upload mode, `backfill --scope activities --mode upload --from 2026-01-01 --apply` uploads the originals of past activities that Intervals doesn’t have.\n- Everything is a dry run until you add `--apply`; the first run prints exactly what it would write.\n\n...\n\nHonest caveats – Garmin’s endpoints are private and may change or rate-limit (the tool backs off and tells you). Writing streams sets `icu_intervals_edited` on the activity and the API can’t reset it. Verified on one account so far (fenix 8, Edge 1040); reports from other devices are very welcome.\n\n...\n\n0.3.0 is out , with the things I wished for after the first days of running it myself:\n\n- Last night’s values right after your morning sync. Sleep, SpO₂, respiration, sleeping HR, HRV detail, sleep stages, Training Readiness and skin temperature now reach Intervals within about ten minutes of the watch syncing, instead of up to four hours later. The bridge no longer re-reads Garmin on a clock: it looks at Intervals’ own record for today (free) and asks Garmin once the official sync has delivered the night. A finished day is read once the watch has synced after midnight, so the day’s totals are complete. On a normal day that’s about 75 Garmin requests instead of ~310.\n- Health Snapshots. Garmin hands partners the snapshot’s values only as a replacement for your resting HR, HRV and SpO₂ – two minutes in a chair overwrite the night. The bridge now puts the night’s values back (same day) and keeps the snapshot in its own fields: heart rate, RMSSD, SDRR, respiration, SpO₂, stress. New chart in the library: search for “Garmin Bridge: Health Snapshot”. Tip: untick SpO2 in Intervals’ Garmin wellness settings – the official sync only ever delivers it from snapshots; the bridge brings the overnight average.\n- More of the night: lowest SpO₂, lowest/highest respiration, Body Battery gained during sleep and Garmin’s HRV baseline band – shown in the existing SpO₂ and HRV detail charts.\n- The watch’s own wellness files. Besides the original activity FITs, the local archive now keeps the device’s original wellness files of every day (all-day HR, respiration per minute, SpO₂ readings, overnight HRV, sleep, skin temperature, Health Snapshots – a few dozen message types Garmin’s API never shows), plus Garmin’s splits per activity and your FTP and running-tolerance history. Nothing of it is needed today; it’s there for whatever we want to extract later, without asking Garmin again.\n- Docker Hub: the image is now also at `futurewebat/garmin-intervals-bridge` (amd64 + arm64), for NAS container managers that only know Docker Hub.\n\n---\n\nTitle: API: upload a file onto an existing activity, and a \"new activity\" webhook for API-key users - Feature Requests - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/api-upload-a-file-onto-an-existing-activity-and-a-new-activity-webhook-for-api-key-users/132953\nPublished: 2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: Andreas_Schnederle-W\nHighlights:\nHi @david – two API wishes from the garmin-intervals-bridge (self-hosted, open source: since Garmin filters the files it hands to partners, it fetches the original FIT from Garmin Connect).\n\n...\n\nToday there are two ways to get Garmin’s complete data into the activity the official import created:\n\n- complete it: write the missing custom fields and streams through the API – but “Reprocess file” drops all of it again, and writing streams sets `icu_intervals_edited` for good;\n- swap it: upload the original as a new activity, carry name, paired workout, RPE, gear etc. over, then delete Garmin’s copy – works, but the activity gets a new id (links and comments are gone), a tombstone stays behind, and a tool has to delete activities, which I’d rather avoid.\n\nWhat would solve it cleanly: an upload that replaces the file of an existing activity and reprocesses it – like “Reprocess file”, just with a new file. For example `POST /api/v1/athlete/{id}/activities?replace={activityId}`, or `PUT /api/v1/activity/{id}/file`. Everything else stays: id, name, description, comments, paired workout, RPE/feel, gear, tags. Guards on your side could be: the athlete’s own activity only, and a file whose start time is within a few minutes of the activity’s. Useful beyond Garmin too, I think: a corrected file, a file merged with a separate HR strap, a better recording from a second device.\n\n...\n\nThis is live now: https://intervals.icu/api-docs.html#post-/api/v1/activity/-id-/file\n\n---\n\nTitle: Intervals.icu News 2026-10-22\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/intervals-icu-news-2026-10-22/133206\nPublished: 2026-10-22T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nThe main menu has been shortened by combining related options: Totals (Totals and Compare), Best Efforts (Power curves and Pace curves), Community (Chats, Groups and Profile)\n\n- We have added Automatic Submaximal Fatigue Testing for monitoring fitness and training status with a simple 3 minute test. The interpretation of the results (do I need to rest?) over time is under development.\n- SIGMA RIDE Sigma bike computers now upload to Intervals.icu!\n- The /fitness page has been redone for phones\n- Added daily floors climbed with Garmin sync\n- INDYA nutrition software for professionals and athletes, now supports Intervals.icu!\n- Crickles does a lot of interesting things with HR data, give it a try\n- Carbs used is now an editable field, and our automatic calculation can be disabled per sport in /settings. Custom activity fields and apps can now set it\n- Better keyboard and screen reader support: Tab reaches the calendar tiles, editable values and icon buttons and Enter opens them, Escape closes the notes and map windows, buttons and images have names, each page has its own browser tab title, and phones can pinch-zoom\n- OAuth client apps can now use PKCE and public clients (no client_secret) are supported\n\n---\n\nTitle: Announcements\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/c/announcements/7\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nLast updated: 30 October 2026 09:39 UTC\n\nOur Submaximal Fatigue Test analysis now answers the “do I need to rest?” question, considering recent tests, resting HR, HRV, sleep and more. iGPSPORT now supports Intervals.icu! Upload completed activities automatica…\n\n...\n\n## Import and download workout and activity FIT files\n\nAuthor: @david\nReplies: 29\nLast updated: 22 October 2026 20:13 UTC\n\nDownload of activities in FIT format is now supported. The downloaded file includes whatever changes you may have made to the power and heartrate data. So you can fix data in Intervals.icu and upload to other services. C…\n\n...\n\n## New compare page with power vs HR chart\n\nAuthor: @david\nReplies: 112\nLast updated: 21 October 2026 17:02 UTC\n\nThere is a new top level menu item for comparing time periods and athletes. It currently only shows power vs heart rate but I intend to add more in future including custom charts (like on the fitness page). The power vs…\n\n...\n\n## All fields from Garmin activity\n\nAuthor: @Povedano\nReplies: 164\nLast updated: 15 October 2026 09:14 UTC\n\nI have been creating some activity custom fields using the powerful “Fit file field” option on intervals.icu custom fields. I’ve been able to extract all this information from my Garmin sessions (F7 + HRM-Pro): They …\n\n...\n\n## Coros support added\n\nAuthor: @david\nReplies: 106\nLast updated: 15 October 2026 03:16 UTC\n\nIntervals.icu can now download completed activities, wellness data (sleep, resting HR) and upload planned workouts to Coros. Look for the Coros box in /settings: If you enable uploading of planned workouts the next w…\n\n...\n\nAuthor: @david\nReplies: 25\nLast updated: 30 September 2026 21:40 UTC\n\nYou can now download all activities in CSV format from the activity calendar. The CSV includes most things including training load, fitness, efficiency etc.. So you can calculate your own things, figure out interesting p…\n\n...\n\n## Upload planned workouts to Garmin Connect\n\nAuthor: @david\nReplies: 196\nLast updated: 15 September 2026 05:44 UTC\n\nYou can link your Garmin Connect account and Intervals.icu will upload your next week of planned workouts. Next time you sync with Garmin Connect the workouts should appear on your device. Go to the /settings page and ti…\n\n---\n\nTitle: Workout builder - Page 58 - Guide - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/workout-builder/1163?page=58\nPublished: 2020-06-16T11:49:37.000Z\nAuthor: Iratiodam\nHighlights:\nDo you mean that un the prompt you can puta instead of Z2 or Z3, if I want the Athlete to maintein in a specific hr range, p.e 140-145 that is not an specific zone, I can do IT? (I’ thought that I was suporter User I’ll chech).\n\nI use to do it with TP, just the Athlete to know wa range of RPE should maintein. The clock shows it, but nothing more.\n\n R2Tom 24 July 2026 11:32 1168 \n\n> Do you mean that un the prompt you can puta instead of Z2 or Z3, if I want the Athlete to maintein in a specific hr range, p.e 140-145 that is not an specific zone, I can do IT? (I’ thought that I was suporter User I’ll chech).\n\nYou can define different zones e.g. X1 140-145bpm X2 145-147 bpm and so on and then define the workout as `- 20m CZX2 HR` (145-147 bpm prescription). Of course you have to define every possible range you want to use as a custom zone range.\n\n...\n\nTo use Custom Zones, the athlete itself also needs ‘supporter’ status, not only the coach! And your avatar does not display the purple supporter star.\n\n...\n\nYes I just tested I can create RPE basd workout in Stryd then it pushes it to Garmin and in my watch there’s a translation to Watt ranges corresponding to the RPE intensity.\n\n...\n\nSee bug report and some suggestions how to improve it here. Activity: workout line is off for distance-based steps\n\nTLDR, intervals.icu simply converts distance to time in that chart based on what it expects the pace to be. This is fine when it’s still in the future.\n\nBut with an completed workout it could (as demonstrated by Garmin) switch to a different algorithm and make the blue line perfect. The algo is not complex and the data is available, just not the priority/dev time. vote that bug report if you think it’s important.\n\n...\n\nBen_Nothling 2 August 2026 04:00 1173 \n\nJust to close this off and for the benefit of someone else looking later, the below syntax did exactly what I wanted it to.\n\nMain Set 5x\n\n- 6m Z4 HR intensity=interval\n- 1m30s intensity=recovery\n\n...\n\nHi everyone, i’m new on this forum but already seems fantastic! \n\nI’m trying to create a Strenght Workout and I wrote this:\n\n\"\n\nWarmup 8m\n\nArnold Press 3x\n\n- Press lap 1m intensity=active Cooldown\n- 2:30 min ramp intensity=cooldown\n\nFly Inverso 3x\n\n- Press lap 1m intensity=active Cooldown\n- 2:30 min ramp intensity=cooldown\n\nPressa Landmine ad un braccio 3x\n\n- Press lap 1m intensity=active Cooldown\n- 2:30 min ramp intensity=cooldown\n\nCurl con manubri su panca inclinata 3x\n\n- Press lap 1m intensity=active Cooldown\n- 2:30 min ramp intensity=cooldown\n\nCurl concentrato 3x\n\n- Press lap 1m intensity=active Cooldown\n- 2:30 min ramp intensity=cooldown\n\nZottman Curl 3x\n\n- Press lap 1m intensity=active Cooldown\n- 2:30 min ramp intensity=cooldown\n\n\"\n\nBut when I import it on my Garmin Instict all the excercises I created have all the same name on the watch: “GO”… How is possibile to read the name of the exercise on the watch?\n\n...\n\nIs there a possibility to convert % of FTP into a text?\n\nI have often in the text an information of the target Watt. See screenshot. But if FTP changes I have to adjust this in every Workout.\n\nSo for example the target is 90% of FTP see the green mark.\n\nAnd the info is 189wa at severel positions. See the yellow marks.\n\nIs there something like [90%] , which converts the value of 90% FTP in a text string?\n\n---\n\nTitle: Cannot combine HR and pace-based intervals in single workout?\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/cannot-combine-hr-and-pace-based-intervals-in-single-workout/130684\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nI have been able to use %HR just fine for interval-less runs:\n\n```auto\nEasy\n- 43m 50-70% HR\n\n```\n\nand these appear as green in the workout visualizer and work fine on garmin:\n\nHowever if I do the same for a warmup/cooldown before/after an interval workout:\n\n```auto\nWarmup\n- 10m intensity=warmup 50-70% HR\n\n5x\n- 6m 4:30-4:40/km Pace\n- 90s intensity=rest\n\nCooldown\n- 10m intensity=cooldown 50-70% HR\n\n```\n\n…it appears as grey (maybe this because it’s not flagged as ‘easy’) and more importantly doesn’t transfer to my garmin (i.e., doesn’t display or tell me if I’m outside the HR range):\n\n(related?) it doesn’t seem to add any load or increase any of the zone durations (top right). indeed if I raise the minutes of the warmup or cooldown it actually reduces the overall training load:\n\n(training load = 1 😃 )\n\nI’ve tried changing them to intervals (1x 10m 50-70% HR), easy (like the previous example), etc, but always gray, always ignored in the zones and reduce the load. (I didn’t test if they went to garmin but I presume not)\n\n...\n\nThere are a multitude of topics regarding this. At the moment, you can’t combine different targets.\nYou can, for the workout load to be correct, define both targets per step and the graph underneath will be correct. Auto follows the priority set, or one of the other options shows you what it is for that target type.\nBut this will not correctly transfer to your device. It will only get the target that is ‘active’ on the workout in Intervals.\n\n> @Combining laps based on power and HR in planned sessions:\n> \n> @MedTechCD , I meanwhile got my eyes on the official Garmin spec for the training API, and it is possible to upload planned activities to Garmin where each step targets its individual intensity and volume (in any combination of type, i.e. time, distance, power, HR etc). I would kindly ask to reconsider this topic. Without knowing interval.icu’s internal limitations in this regard, it would be an awesome improvement in expressiveness of the planned workouts; and with a bit of luck involve very …\n\n...\n\nThis issue has been (almost) fixed for me as per the latest updates in Combining laps based on power and HR in planned sessions - #28 by eva - such mixed workouts sync correctly to my garmin. Great! I have become a “supporter” to show my appreciation ❤\n\nthat issue is now ‘resolved’ but the predicted load issue of such mixed pace/HR workouts is still outstanding for me. i don’t think the fix has been ‘officially’ finished yet so this might be still to come, but yeah - noticed one other thing also!\n\n...\n\nBut be aware that you can manually override the load for future events. I plan to use that feature to fix the load for sessions with HR via the API. As I only use HR for Z1/Z2 personally, the regression is nicely linear (in my data, YMMV).\n\n...\n\nThe load calculation for mixed HR/pace workouts is now fixed too. The builder sums load across both the HR- and pace-based steps, so HR warmup/cooldown steps now contribute properly. That should also clear up the inflated compliance scores and the duplicate-workout pairing you were seeing, since the planned and completed loads now line up. Give it a try on your mixed workouts and let me know if anything still looks off.\n\n...\n\nThis is fixed now. Now it respects the target selection first and uses steps target only on “auto” mode.\n\n...\n\nAuthor: @eva\nPost date: 21 October 2026 06:22 UTC\n\n> @axlhm:\n> \n> I believe there is a recent change creating a bug on how load is calculated on workout editor /planned workouts.\n\nActually this wasn’t fixed, the piece of code which would fix this didn’t get through!\nNow it should really be fixed and at the same time fixed the calculation of NP in the mixed workouts.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Latest\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/latest?no_definitions=true&page=1\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nReplies: 112\nLast updated: 21 October 2026 17:02 UTC\n\nThere is a new top level menu item for comparing time periods and athletes. It currently only shows power vs heart rate but I intend to add more in future including custom charts (like on the fitness page). The power vs…\n\n...\n\nWatch: Garmin Fenix 8 I’m pushing structured running workouts via the Intervals.icu API (warmup / interval repeats with absolute pace-range targets, e.g. 4:30-4:35/km Pace / recovery / cooldown) to Garmin Connect, then …\n\n---\n\nTitle: API access to Intervals.icu\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/api-access-to-intervals-icu/609?page=38\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n1. Will Garmin-sourced activities be returned as stubs (like Strava) or omitted entirely?\n2. Are wellness fields (HRV, sleep, resting HR) synced from Garmin also affected?\n3. Derived values (eFTP, fitness/fatigue, thresholds) computed from Garmin activities — still available?\n4. Activities uploaded manually as FIT files (originally recorded on a Garmin) — treated as Garmin data?\n5. Planned workouts pushed via API and synced to the device — unaffected?\n6. Any expected timeline?\n\n...\n\nThanks David – “uploads are likely ok” is the important bit. For anyone who wants to be ready before the block lands: switch the Garmin import off in Intervals and let garmin-intervals-bridge upload the original FIT of every new activity as your own file. Verified today on my account: Intervals computes Stamina streams, Recovery Time, VO₂max, Performance Condition, Training Effect and the other custom items from the complete file itself – nothing is flagged as edited, and the activity is a plain upload, not Garmin API data. Wellness (SpO₂, sleep, HRV, Body Battery, nutrition …) works the same way. Docs: GitHub - futureweb/garmin-intervals-bridge: Puts back what Garmin strips from the FIT files it sends to Intervals.icu - and syncs the wellness data the official sync misses · GitHub → “Upload mode”.\n\n---\n\nTitle: API access to Intervals.icu\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/api-access-to-intervals-icu/609?page=43\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nRight now the API is still returning data for prior Garmin activities. Will Garmin data returned by the intervals.icu API become more like how Strava sourced data is handled, where _ no details _ are returned? Or will just specific Garmin training status metrics and vo2 max no longer be available?\n\n...\n\nI can confirm that a .fit file downloaded using a personal API key is identical to the one that can be downloaded manually from the Connect website. It contains all Garmin-specific data fields.\n\n...\n\nUnfortunately that is likely to be the case. We hope to be able to return a stub with the Garmin ID, like we do for Strava, to help people and apps with their own connections to Garmin. We are talking to Garmin and will be sorting out all of the details over the next weeks or months.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"mcporter call exa.web_search_exa query='site:forum.intervals.icu \\\"October 2026\\\" \\\"target\\\" \\\"auto\\\" \\\"Garmin\\\" workout_doc' numResults=5\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"mcporter call exa.web_search_exa query='site:forum.intervals.icu \\\"October 2026\\\" \\\"target\\\" \\\"auto\\\" \\\"Garmin\\\" workout_doc' numResults=5\"","aggregated_output":"Title: Capture carbs, protein and fat intake\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/capture-carbs-protein-and-fat-intake/122594\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nWe have added wellness fields for carbs, protein and fat, all in grams:\n\nYou can add these to the wellness capture dialog, click “Fields” at the bottom:\n\nAnd Options → Wellness to display them on the calendar:\n\nYou can also plot them on the /fitness page.\n\n...\n\n> Answering my own question for anyone who finds this: Garmin doesn’t pass the Connect+ food log to Intervals, so I built a self-hosted tool that does – it writes calories, carbs, protein and fat from Garmin Connect into these native fields once the day is over. The chart “Garmin Bridge: Nutrition macros” in the library shows them stacked by energy share. https://forum.intervals.icu/t/132863\n\n---\n\nTitle: Garmin-intervals-bridge – your complete Garmin FIT files and wellness data in Intervals.icu, with or without the official Garmin integration (now also with Garmin’s Personal API)\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/garmin-intervals-bridge-your-complete-garmin-fit-files-and-wellness-data-in-intervals-icu-with-or-without-the-official-garmin-integration-now-also-with-garmin-s-personal-api/132863?page=5\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n@MedTechCD – thanks for the detailed report, you found a real gap! Your config is fine. The automatic advice waited for the night the bridge reads from Garmin – and with Garmin only on the bike, Garmin never has one (that’s the “Garmin has no sleep for 2026-10-17 yet” in your log), so the advice never got its turn. Run by hand it doesn’t wait for Garmin, which is why that works.\n\nFixed for the next version: when Garmin hasn’t delivered a night in the past week, the advice takes today’s HRV and resting HR from Intervals as soon as they’re there – your Kubios values included. Until then, the command you already use does the job:\n\n```auto\npy -m garmin_intervals_bridge imready4 --apply\n\n```\n\n...\n\n0.11.4 – catching up after a pause, and clearer messages\n\nCatching up after a pause. Your computer is only on now and then, a server was down, or Garmin wanted a new sign-in for a few days? The bridge now reads back to where its last run left off, up to 14 days. Activities and wellness days in between are completed as if it had run all along. A run cut short (Garmin limits it, the computer goes off) is carried on by the next one.\n\nFixed\n\n- ImReady4 and HRV-trend advice now also run automatically without a Garmin watch at night. When Garmin hasn’t had a night for a week, the advice takes today’s HRV and resting HR from Intervals as soon as they’re there (@MedTechCD).\n- Windows: the bridge no longer runs in a PowerShell window opened as administrator, and says why. Folders created there were closed to the background bridge – the PermissionError @Okocha ran into. If you really want it: `BRIDGE_ALLOW_ADMIN=true`. And when `schtasks` fails, you now see its own message.\n- The web page after an update: a page opened with `ui` before the update notices that it’s older than what’s installed and tells you to reopen it (@Lukasz_Pludowski).\n- A failed Garmin sign-in says what it was about. Only wrong credentials say “check e-mail and password”; when Garmin didn’t let the sign-in through, it says so – and that the password isn’t the problem.\n- Pasting the API key in setup on Windows: Ctrl+V in Windows Terminal, a right-click (on a trackpad a two-finger tap) in the classic console (@Andy_Drake).\n\n...\n\nFor the activities since Garmin started filtering it: after the update, a backfill --scope activities --from 2026-10-01 --apply fills them in. Only missing values are added, nothing you typed in is overwritten.\n\n...\n\nFor activities you already have: those in the last few days get one more look by themselves. For the past since Garmin started filtering, a backfill fills them in:\n\n```auto\ngarmin-intervals-bridge backfill --scope activities --from 2026-10-01 --apply\n\n---\n\nTitle: Latest\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/latest?no_definitions=true&page=1\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n## Structured pace-range target shows as single average on Garmin Fenix 8 (worked correctly until recently)\n\nAuthor: @Adriano_Sycyliano\nReplies: 2\nLast updated: 21 October 2026 14:06 UTC\n\nWatch: Garmin Fenix 8 I’m pushing structured running workouts via the Intervals.icu API (warmup / interval repeats with absolute pace-range targets, e.g. 4:30-4:35/km Pace / recovery / cooldown) to Garmin Connect, then …\n\n---\n\nTitle: Cannot combine HR and pace-based intervals in single workout?\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/cannot-combine-hr-and-pace-based-intervals-in-single-workout/130684\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nThere are a multitude of topics regarding this. At the moment, you can’t combine different targets.\nYou can, for the workout load to be correct, define both targets per step and the graph underneath will be correct. Auto follows the priority set, or one of the other options shows you what it is for that target type.\nBut this will not correctly transfer to your device. It will only get the target that is ‘active’ on the workout in Intervals.\n\n> @Combining laps based on power and HR in planned sessions:\n> \n> @MedTechCD , I meanwhile got my eyes on the official Garmin spec for the training API, and it is possible to upload planned activities to Garmin where each step targets its individual intensity and volume (in any combination of type, i.e. time, distance, power, HR etc). I would kindly ask to reconsider this topic. Without knowing interval.icu’s internal limitations in this regard, it would be an awesome improvement in expressiveness of the planned workouts; and with a bit of luck involve very …\n\n...\n\n> @MedTechCD:\n> \n> You can, for the workout load to be correct, define both targets per step and the graph underneath will be correct. Auto follows the priority set, or one of the other options shows you what it is for that target type.\n\nInteresting, but it seems like maybe that could be improved? like ‘auto’ to me should be able to calculate load by HR or pace as the step demands. Priority set should only come into it if you have multiple targets per step and it needs to choose.\n\nIn my case I don’t want to use a pace target for my warmup/cooldown, as it varies depending on the intensity of the workout/my condition (but my target HR remains the same).\n\n...\n\nThis issue has been (almost) fixed for me as per the latest updates in Combining laps based on power and HR in planned sessions - #28 by eva - such mixed workouts sync correctly to my garmin. Great! I have become a “supporter” to show my appreciation ❤\n\n...\n\nThe load calculation for mixed HR/pace workouts is now fixed too. The builder sums load across both the HR- and pace-based steps, so HR warmup/cooldown steps now contribute properly. That should also clear up the inflated compliance scores and the duplicate-workout pairing you were seeing, since the planned and completed loads now line up. Give it a try on your mixed workouts and let me know if anything still looks off.\n\n...\n\nThe toggle on “Auto/HR”/Pace\" on the workout seems to no longer work when two of “Pace”, “Power” and “HR” is given, load doesn’t change. Perhaps defaulting to settings Training Load Priority? (I have Pace, HR, Power selected there)\n\n...\n\nAuthor: @eva\nPost date: 14 August 2026 09:56 UTC\n\n> @axlhm:\n> \n> The toggle on “Auto/HR”/Pace\" on the workout seems to no longer work when two of “Pace”, “Power” and “HR” is given, load doesn’t change.\n\nThis is fixed now. Now it respects the target selection first and uses steps target only on “auto” mode.\n\n...\n\n> @axlhm:\n> \n> I believe there is a recent change creating a bug on how load is calculated on workout editor /planned workouts.\n\nActually this wasn’t fixed, the piece of code which would fix this didn’t get through!\nNow it should really be fixed and at the same time fixed the calculation of NP in the mixed workouts.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Futureweb Intervals MCP – open-source MCP server for ChatGPT & Claude: coaching analytics, all custom fields & streams, sign in with Intervals.icu\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/futureweb-intervals-mcp-open-source-mcp-server-for-chatgpt-claude-coaching-analytics-all-custom-fields-streams-sign-in-with-intervals-icu/132899\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n- “Analyse yesterday’s workout against the plan.” Planned vs executed per step, also with Garmin auto-laps, extended rides and deleted events; time beyond the plan is reported separately.\n- “How is my training load and intensity distribution over the last 4 weeks?” Acute:chronic ratio, monotony and strain, polarization index, durability.\n- “How am I recovering?” HRV, resting HR, sleep and readiness against your personal baselines. Values that have not arrived yet for today (e.g. before your morning sync) are flagged as missing, not treated as normal.\n- “What happens to my form if I add a 5 h ride on Saturday and taper for the race on the 29th?” What-if load projection with form on race day and plan checks.\n- “How does my HR drift after 1,500 kJ?” Long-ride fatigue profile in kJ and kJ/kg, submax test trends, power meter comparison across rides (never an automatic correction factor).\n- “How was my fueling?” Carbs used vs ingested per hour, sweat loss, W′ balance and weather in the activity report, earlier rides on the same route.\n\n...\n\n- Every custom field and stream you defined in Intervals.icu, with names and units. Together with the Garmin Intervals Bridge that includes stamina, training effect, recovery time, VO₂max, running dynamics, sleep and HRV details.\n- Safe by default. Read-only unless you enable writes. Writes (workouts, notes) are validated first, support a dry run, refuse duplicates on the same day and read back what Intervals.icu actually stored. Deletions need an explicit confirmation of the previewed events.\n- Self-hosted. Your data only travels between your server or computer, Intervals.icu and your AI assistant.\n- Sign in with Intervals.icu for ChatGPT and Claude.ai: a built-in OAuth 2.1 server, so only your athlete can connect (or, optionally, a few friends with their own data).\n\n...\n\n# 🚴 Threshold Ride – October 9, 2026\n\nCanyon Ultimate CFR Di2 | 1:27:06 moving | 41.5 km | 333 m elevation | Training Load 125 | IF 0.93\n\n## Key Findings\n\n- Workout successfully executed: All 7 planned steps matched, including 3×10-minute threshold intervals. All three work blocks were within the ±5% target tolerance.\n- Excellent power consistency: 249 / 249 / 253 W, with no meaningful power fade. Slightly above the exact prescribed targets.\n- Progressive cardiovascular response: Average HR increased from 153 to 160 bpm across the work intervals.\n- Additional training: 22:07 beyond the planned workout, including a 50-second effort averaging 432 W. This extra work was correctly excluded from workout compliance.\n- Garmin Training Effect: Aerobic 3.9, anaerobic 1.6 — classified as Threshold.\n\n## Planned vs. Executed\n\n| Interval | Target | Actual | Time in range* | HR drift |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 10 min #1 | 238–242 W | 249 W | 54% | +7.4% |\n| 10 min #2 | 240–244 W | 249 W | 47% | +3.5% |\n| 10 min #3 | 242–246 W | 253 W | 45% | +4.9% |\n| Extra effort | Not planned | 432 W / 50 s | — | — |\n\nTime within the target range, allowing ±5%. HR drift represents the reported Power decoupling within each work interval.\n\n...\n\n## VO₂max Readiness Check — October 10, 2026\n\nVerdict:\n\nProvisionally ready\n\nfor a VO₂max session on Monday, October 12. I would keep Sunday easy or rest, then recheck recovery before the harder workout.\n\n### Why now?\n\n| Metric | Current | Context |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| HRV | 38 ms | Baseline 37.5 ms |\n| Resting HR | 49 bpm | Baseline 51 bpm |\n| Sleep | 7h 48m | Sleep score 85 |\n| Garmin Readiness | 77/100 | Body Battery max 87 |\n| CTL / ATL | 63.7 / 64.1 | Form −0.4 |\n| Acute:Chronic ratio | 1.06 | 7-day load 560 |\n\nRecovery markers are reassuring, but Friday’s threshold ride added 125 training load, following other substantial sessions earlier in the week. Monday provides more recovery time than Sunday.\n\nThe calendar and training plan currently contain no workouts scheduled for October 11–12. Some Garmin recovery fields are not yet synced, so this remains a conditional recommendation.\n\n### Proposed workout — Monday, October 12\n\nCurrent Ride FTP: 234 W; VO₂max Zone 5: 246–280 W.\n\n| Block | Duration | Power target |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Progressive warm-up | 17 min | 50–75% FTP (117–176 W) |\n| VO₂max × 5 | 3 min each | 115% FTP (~269 W) |\n| Recovery × 4, between efforts | 3 min each | 50% FTP (~117 W) |\n| Cool-down | 10 min | 55→40% FTP (129→94 W) |\n| Total | 54 min | 15 min high-intensity work |\n\nAim for 90–105 rpm during the efforts, keeping the first repeat controlled rather than starting with a sprint.\n\nValidation passed: `validate_workout` returned `ok: true`, with zero errors, zero warnings and no calendar conflict. The structured workout contains 11 expanded steps and is Garmin-compatible according to the MCP validator.\n\n### Calendar Dry-Run Preview\n\nValidated — nothing written\n\nI called `add_or_update_event(dry_run=true)` successfully.\n\n| Field | Preview |\n| --- | --- |\n| Date | Monday, October 12, 2026 |\n| Name | VO2max 5x3 min - Ultimate |\n| Sport | Ride |\n| Category | WORKOUT |\n| Duration | 54 minutes |\n| Location | Outdoor |\n| Targets | %FTP, with cadence cues |\n| Duplicate check | No conflict |\n\nThe preview would create a new calendar event using `POST /athlete/{athlete_id}/events`. Its default start field is `2026-10-12T00:00:00`, representing a date-only calendar placement, not a requested midnight training time.\n\nThe server explicitly confirmed:\n\n> Dry run: nothing was written.\n\n...\n\n- Estimated workout IF: 0.86\n- Estimated workout load: 67\n\n...\n\nThe workout load is an\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
